{
  "mn7:0.1": "Middle Discourses 7 ",
  "mn7:0.2": "The Simile of the Cloth ",
  "mn7:1.1": "So I have heard. ",
  "mn7:1.2": "At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. ",
  "mn7:1.3": "There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, ",
  "mn7:1.4": "“Mendicants!” ",
  "mn7:1.5": "“Venerable sir,” they replied. ",
  "mn7:1.6": "The Buddha said this: ",
  "mn7:2.1": "“Suppose, mendicants, there was a cloth that was dirty and soiled. ",
  "mn7:2.2": "No matter what dye the dyer applied—whether blue or yellow or red or magenta—it would look poorly dyed and impure in color. ",
  "mn7:2.3": "Why is that? ",
  "mn7:2.4": "Because of the impurity of the cloth. ",
  "mn7:2.5": "In the same way, when the mind is corrupt, a bad destiny is to be expected. ",
  "mn7:2.6": "Suppose there was a cloth that was pure and clean. ",
  "mn7:2.7": "No matter what dye the dyer applied—whether blue or yellow or red or magenta—it would look well dyed and pure in color. ",
  "mn7:2.8": "Why is that? ",
  "mn7:2.9": "Because of the purity of the cloth. ",
  "mn7:2.10": "In the same way, when the mind isn’t corrupt, a good destiny is to be expected. ",
  "mn7:3.1": "And what are the corruptions of the mind? ",
  "mn7:3.2": "Covetousness and immoral greed, ill will, anger, acrimony, disdain, contempt, jealousy, stinginess, deceit, deviousness, obstinacy, aggression, conceit, arrogance, vanity, and negligence are corruptions of the mind. ",
  "mn7:4.1": "A mendic